<p class="state-label">Before the second transfer · after the first transfer and controlled reset</p><div class="setup-flow" role="img" aria-label="A full Mekanism cube supplies a nearly full idle dropper first, followed by an empty later consumer."><div class="flow-card"><small>Source</small><strong>Mekanism cube</strong><span>Refilled to its full starting energy</span></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="flow-card"><small>First destination</small><strong>Idle dropper</strong><span>99,999 / 100,000 FE · 1 FE gap</span></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="flow-card"><small>Later destination</small><strong>Empty consumer</strong><span>Needs 100,000 FE</span></div></div>
<p>The diagram shows the <strong>second-pass starting state</strong>, not the initial 25-FE gap. The controlled fixture proceeds in three steps:</p>
<ol class="state-timeline"><li><strong>Initial state:</strong> a full 100,000-FE dropper performs a real item drop, spending 25 FE. The fixture asserts <strong>99,975 FE</strong> remain: a 25-FE gap.</li><li><strong>First SFM transfer:</strong> the dropper requests 25 FE, but the pinned adapter extracts and delivers only <strong>24 FE</strong>. It reaches <strong>99,999 FE</strong>: a one-FE gap. The later consumer receives 100,000 FE in both builds.</li><li><strong>Controlled reset, then second transfer:</strong> the fixture refills the cube and drains the later consumer, leaving the idle first dropper at 99,999 FE. Its one-FE request returns zero. The baseline starves the later consumer; the candidate supplies 100,000 FE.</li></ol>
<p class="small-note">These exact amounts come from the <a href="#energy-asset-ordered-dropper-first-sfml">program evidence</a> and <a href="#impact-visual-fixture">fixture assertions and pass observations</a>. A rounded GUI tooltip cannot distinguish 99,999 from 100,000 FE.</p>
<p class="small-note">This reproduction uses Minecraft 1.21.1, NeoForge 21.1.206, Mekanism 10.7.4.60 and Just Dire Things 1.5.7, with 2.5 joules per FE. It characterizes the same kind of quantity-sensitive transfer failure using known, pinned blocks.</p>
