<section class="reading" id="rounding">
<span class="section-number">02 · Following the arithmetic</span><h2>Why 25 FE becomes 24—and one becomes zero</h2>
<p>Mekanism stores integer joules. Its pinned Forge Energy adapter converts the requested FE amount into joules, simulates extraction, and converts the available amount to whole FE and back before executing. At 2.5 J per FE, those integer conversions can discard a fraction.</p>
<div class="math-grid"><div class="math-card"><h3>First transfer · request 25 FE</h3><div class="math-step">25 FE × 2.5 = 62.5 J<br><b>Truncated to 62 J</b></div><div class="math-step">62 J ÷ 2.5 = 24.8 FE<br><b>Truncated to 24 FE</b></div><div class="math-step">24 FE × 2.5 = <b>60 J</b></div><p class="math-result">24 FE extracted · 1 FE still missing</p></div><div class="math-card refusal"><h3>Second transfer · request 1 FE</h3><div class="math-step">1 FE × 2.5 = 2.5 J<br><b>Truncated to 2 J</b></div><div class="math-step">2 J ÷ 2.5 = 0.8 FE<br><b>Truncated to 0 FE</b></div><div class="math-step">0 FE × 2.5 = <b>0 J</b></div><p class="math-result">Zero FE extracted · source still full</p></div></div>
<p>This arithmetic follows the pinned adapter’s bytecode: <code>IEnergyConversion.convertFrom</code> multiplies by the conversion factor, <code>MathUtils.clampToLong</code> uses a truncating cast, and <code>ForgeEnergyIntegration.convertToAndBack</code> checks the whole-FE amount before execution. The complete disassembly is retained in the technical appendix.</p>
<blockquote class="pullquote">“This request returned nothing” does not mean “this source has nothing left.”</blockquote>
<p>A larger request can still succeed. The empty later consumer asks for 100,000 FE, which maps cleanly to 250,000 J. The energy exists; the failure is specific to the smaller quantity.</p>
</section>
<section class="reading" id="fix">
<span class="section-number">03 · Keeping the next destination in play</span><h2>A smaller decision at the failure branch</h2>
<p>SFM first probes what a destination can accept, then calls <code>source.extract(amountAvailableToMove)</code> for the real transfer. In the old <code>OutputStatement.moveTo</code> branch, an empty extraction marked the whole input slot done. The destination loop then stopped considering that source for the remaining destinations.</p>
<div class="code-pair"><div><h3>4.34 · retire the source</h3><pre><code>{{ before_branch }}</code></pre></div><div><h3>4.35 candidate · skip this pair</h3><pre><code>{{ after_branch }}</code></pre></div></div>
<p class="code-label">Source excerpts from the actual before/after <code>OutputStatement</code>. Comments and trace logging are omitted; the executable branch lines are unchanged.</p>
<p>The candidate removes <code>source.setDone()</code> from this branch. It returns from the current source/destination attempt while leaving the source eligible for a later destination. In this setup, that means a failed one-FE request no longer blocks the successful 100,000-FE request behind it.</p>
<h3>What else changes when the source stays eligible?</h3>
<p><code>setDone()</code> is a <strong>state mutation</strong>, not a check: it sets the input slot’s cached <code>done</code> flag. The old call prevented subsequent destinations—and later <code>OUTPUT</code> statements reusing that input—from asking it again. Treating a zero extraction as exhaustion avoided further handler calls; that explanation is inferred from the control flow, not a proven historical design rationale. <a href="#issue610-output-statement">Before/after source</a> · <a href="#impact-input-state">input-state and cache evidence</a>.</p>
<p>The candidate returns from the refused pair and advances to the next output slot. An input that still reports resources and has budget can therefore be tried again at a different quantity. This can add capability reads, simulated insertion and real extraction calls for sources that keep refusing. The collected destination iterator is finite; there is no new retry loop for the same pair. Later output statements can make further attempts while the input remains in scope, so this is a bound per visited destination, not a fixed total for every program or an assurance against a slow handler.</p>
<p>Other guards remain: resource-type and destination-filter checks, retention obligations, input/output quantity limits and stack limits still run. <code>isDone()</code> still stops empty or removed slots, rejected resources and exhausted trackers; pooled input initialization resets the flag. The cached state lasts with the gathered input until its slots are freed, rather than permanently disabling a machine. Simulation-only exploration takes its existing separate branch. <a href="#impact-input-state">State lifetime</a> · <a href="#impact-input-cache">cache/freeing</a> · <a href="#issue610-output-statement">guards and simulation branch</a>.</p>
<p>The change does not rewrite label or round-robin selection. It can change which already-selected later destinations get serviced, and therefore observed distribution and handler-call counts. It does not promise fairness, unchanged timing or validation of every <code>ROUND ROBIN</code> configuration.</p>
<p>A zero extraction returns before real insertion and transfer-budget accounting. Successful attempts still track the actual moved amount. The existing simulation/remainder checks remain, including resource-loss diagnostics when a handler’s actual insertion disagrees with its simulation; this fix does not make arbitrary third-party handlers safe. Conservation and bounded refusal are established for the tested fixtures, not every resource or mod. <a href="#issue610-output-statement">Transfer and remainder paths</a> · <a href="#energy-asset-resource-test-java">resource test source</a>.</p>
<p class="small-note">The selected resource test checks 256 refusing destinations for synthetic item, fluid and FE handlers, unchanged quantities and budgets, initially empty sources and quantity/retention limits. The real-mod cases separately cover the pinned energy adapter, ordering, EACH SIDE and retain controls. These tests do not benchmark large networks, establish all-mod behavior, or give real item/fluid GUI acceptance.</p>
</section>
