<h3 id="recipe-namespace">Recipe IDs now use SFM’s namespace</h3>
<p>The recipe-namespace concern is reported in <a href="https://github.com/TeamDman/SuperFactoryManager/issues/621">#621</a> and tracked by <a href="https://github.com/TeamDman/SuperFactoryManager/issues/527">#527</a>. A concrete Minecraft 1.21.1 archive example:</p>
<pre class="recipe-path-diff"><code><span class="removed-line">− data/minecraft/recipe/disk_reset.json</span>
<span class="fixed-line">+ data/sfm/recipe/disk_reset.json</span></code></pre>
<p>The recipe ID changes from <code>minecraft:disk_reset</code> to <code>sfm:disk_reset</code>. Its JSON is <strong>byte-for-byte unchanged</strong> in these two archives; <code>type</code> names the serializer and was already SFM’s:</p>
<pre class="json-source"><code>{
  <span class="json-key">"type"</span>: <span class="json-string">"sfm:disk_reset"</span>,
  <span class="json-key">"category"</span>: <span class="json-string">"misc"</span>
}</code></pre>
<p>A related JSON reference really does change. In <code>advancement/recipes/misc/tough_cable_horizontal.json</code>, the complete <code>criteria.has_the_recipe.conditions</code> object is:</p>
<div class="code-pair recipe-reference"><div><h3>4.34 · old recipe ID</h3><pre class="json-source"><code>{
  <span class="json-key">"recipe"</span>: <span class="json-string">"minecraft:tough_cable_horizontal"</span>
}</code></pre></div><div><h3>4.35 candidate · SFM recipe ID</h3><pre class="json-source"><code>{
  <span class="json-key">"recipe"</span>: <span class="json-string">"sfm:tough_cable_horizontal"</span>
}</code></pre></div></div>
<p class="code-label">Read from the frozen published 4.34 baseline and candidate 4.35 JARs for Minecraft 1.21.1. The advancement also moves into <code>data/sfm/</code> and its reward recipe reference changes; vanilla parent/trigger identifiers remain unchanged. <a href="#jar-1-21-1">Paired archive provenance</a> · <a href="#recipe-example-provenance">example evidence</a>.</p>
<p>The candidate also saves named recipe IDs under <code>sfm:</code> instead of <code>minecraft:</code>. Across the ten archives, 123 recipe payloads were relocated with their JSON contents unchanged. Advancement recipe references adopt the SFM namespace; vanilla tags stay under <code>minecraft</code>.</p><p>Datapacks and commands referencing the old <code>minecraft:</code> IDs must switch to <code>sfm:</code>. This recipe-namespace change is tracked in <a href="https://github.com/TeamDman/SuperFactoryManager/issues/527">#527</a> and reported in <a href="https://github.com/TeamDman/SuperFactoryManager/issues/621">#621</a>. Previously unlocked recipe IDs may need unlocking again.</p>
</section>
<section class="reading" id="scope">
<span class="section-number">06 · Using the finding</span><h2>Validation scope and a practical workaround</h2>
<p>This is evidence for a release candidate, not an announcement of a published release. It demonstrates the quantity-sensitive source-progress fix in the pinned Mekanism/Just Dire Things setup and the selected server regression cases. It does not establish runtime acceptance across all ten packaged versions.</p>
<p>There are several community energy reports. <a href="https://github.com/TeamDman/SuperFactoryManager/issues/528">#528</a> describes Productive Bees centrifuges intermittently losing power; the October 10 follow-up in <a href="https://github.com/TeamDman/SuperFactoryManager/issues/610">#610</a> records another heated-centrifuge setup. Their symptoms do not establish identical causes. Our pinned reproduction uses Mekanism and Just Dire Things, with no Productive Bees. Direct Productive Bees reproduction and the follow-up’s exact versions/configuration remain open.</p>
<div class="workaround"><h3>For existing 4.34 programs</h3><p>Consider visiting the bulk consumer before the nearly full machine. The healthy-first control passes in this reproduction: an awkward small request cannot starve a destination that has already received its transfer. Changing order changes priority, so check the result in your own setup. <a href="https://github.com/TeamDman/SuperFactoryManager/issues/610">#610’s measured controls and workaround discussion</a> distinguish this ordering control from an illustrative RETAIN target; neither validates a Productive Bees workaround. RETAIN controls a target stored amount, whereas OUTPUT 1000 is only an upper transfer limit and still allows a one-FE request.</p></div>
<p>The general lesson is useful beyond this one pack: an adapter’s refusal of a particular quantity should not automatically retire a source that can still satisfy another destination. The candidate makes that distinction at the point where SFM decides whether to keep moving.</p>
<p class="endnote">All underlying captures, source comparisons, test records and archive identities are retained in this report and its downloadable evidence. No game build or capture was rerun to prepare this article.</p>
